WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.120
[music]

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.820
It's 4:30 in the morning

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.220
on a remote mountaintop in the Chilean desert.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.180
Pink Floyd blares through Ian Shelton's headphones

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.320
as he meticulously maneuvers a 10-inch telescope

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.600
at Las Campanas Observatory.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.440
It's February 24, 1987,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.720
and Shelton, a telescope operator,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.480
is having a rough night.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.520
The observatory's sliding roof door isn't working

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.000
when he arrives.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.460
After finally getting it open, he spends three hours

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.950
painstakingly taking a picture of the

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.560
Large Magellanic Cloud,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.380
a galaxy that orbits the Milky Way.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.760
Suddenly a gust of wind slams the door closed,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:43.660
[slam]

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.560
and everything goes black.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.520
Shelton takes the hint.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.360
Shelton: "Maybe I'll just call it a night."

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.480
But things are about to look up.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.220
He takes the eight-by-ten photographic plate

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.220
to the darkroom and develops it.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.840
Checking the exposure,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.900
he immediately notices something odd.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.260
Shelton: "There's this extra star that was

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.160
brighter effectively than all the other ones.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.220
I figured why didn't I use that one

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.520
to line up the telescope?

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.660
Because it wasn't there.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.720
It shouldn't be there."

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.980
Shelton checks it against the previous night's plate.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.880
Nope, definitely not supposed to be there.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.600
He grabs his jacket, steps outside and looks up.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.560
Sure enough, there it is --

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.260
a bright point of light near the tarantula nebula.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.000
Shelton: "For me, I was just quite surprised

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.140
to the point of a bit of disbelief."

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.100
So, Shelton heads down the road

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.100
to check with the guys at another telescope.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:32.820
[door opening]

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.720
He casually asks fellow astronomer Barry Madore:

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.380
Shelton: "Well what would you say about a

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.200
a fifth magnitude object in the LMC?"

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.000
Madore does the math in his head.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.000
Shelton: "And he says....ehh supernova

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.060
and then he looks at me, you know does a double take,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.340
You're kidding!"

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.920
That's when telescope operator

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.580
Oscar Duhalde chimes in.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.600
Shelton: "He says, 'Yes, yes, I saw something there, too.'

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.080
He was probably the first person to see naked eye.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.160
Around 166,000 light-years away,

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.540
a hefty star had gone supernova.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.820
It ran out of fuel,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.840
and its core collapsed, starting a shock wave.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.900
Ghostly particles called neutrinos helped push

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.920
the shock wave to the star's surface,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:11.340
and it exploded.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.600
The detonation spewed the star's

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.660
chemical gets into space.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.240
Its end would churn up gaseous building blocks

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.680
for stellar construction.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.780
Shelton and his colleagues were seeing this distant

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.920
light show 166,000 years later --

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.320
as close to real time as you could get.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.140
The last time someone had directly observed

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.500
a supernova with the naked eye was in 1604.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.040
So the crew at Las Campanas has to get the word out.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.020
Except no one knows the protocol for

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.820
notifying the International Astronomical Union

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:44.960
about a supernova.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.380
Shelton digs up an old article

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.180
in Sky and Telescope magazine.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.000
Shelton: "A simple half page, quarter page little thing,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.440
'How to report a discovery.'"

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.440
He tries calling the IAU office in Massachusetts.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.500
[ringing]

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.160
Shelton: "It rang and rang. But it just never picked up."

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.400
So they go to plan B: Send a telegram.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.360
They scribble a note and instructions on a piece of paper.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.180
A driver sets off for the closest town, La Serena,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.600
about a hundred kilometers away

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.380
in search of a telex machine,

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.520
the equivalent of email today.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.120
For the astronomy community, it was a big, big news.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.600
Shelton: "And I guess everybody thought

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.100
well now this is the big one."

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.260
Shelton's supernova became known as 1987A.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.520
Its proximity to earth meant it could be

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:30.780
viewed with the naked eye

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.540
from the southern hemisphere -- a rare treat.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.160
Stars go supernova all the time in the universe,

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:40.560
but 1987A confirmed a lot of things that

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.580
astronomers had theorized about the death of stars.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.520
It also marked the first supernova where

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.240
astronomers could pinpoint

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.460
the identity of the progenitor star,

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.540
Sanduleak -69 202.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.940
And it was the first time researchers detected

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.520
neutrinos from a stellar explosion.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.500
It's been 30 years since Shelton captured

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.140
1987A's fiery blast.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.400
Some scientists are still learning things from

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.600
the supernova and remains it left behind.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.260
In the meantime, Shelton and others await

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.200
their next opportunity to see a star explode.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:32.620
[music]

